# Next.js Best Practices

This document outlines best practices for Next.js development in our project, focusing on app router, data fetching, page optimization, and project structure.

## App Router Structure

- Use the App Router directory structure for new features
- Organize by feature/module, not by file type
- Keep page components in `page.tsx` files
- Place shared layouts in `layout.tsx` files
- Use loading states with `loading.tsx`
- Implement error boundaries with `error.tsx`

## Data Fetching

- Prefer React Server Components (RSC) for data fetching
- Use `fetch` with appropriate cache settings for server components
- For client components, use React Query or SWR for data fetching
- Implement proper error handling and loading states

## Performance Optimization

- Use Image component for optimized images
- Implement proper code splitting with dynamic imports
- Utilize Next.js built-in optimizations (`next/font`, etc.)
- Apply proper caching strategies with `fetch`

## Project Organization

- Place reusable components in `components/` directories
- Keep utility functions in `utils/` or `lib/` directories
- Store types in `types/` directories
- Organize API routes logically in the `api` directory

## Monorepo Considerations

- Follow the workspace pattern for shared code
- Use absolute imports for better code organization
- Leverage Next.js module path aliases

- Utilize Next.js 15's new features like the App Router for improved performance and routing.
- Implement server-side rendering (SSR) for better SEO and initial load times.
- Use `getStaticProps` and `getStaticPaths` for static generation where applicable.
- Leverage Next.js built-in image optimization with the `next/image` component. 
